我跟你说，你在编织最污的叙事网络方面真的很有天赋，我从来没见过，这对我来说总是很震撼。

00:44:45.800 --> 00:44:54.840
I'm telling you, you have a real talent for spinning the dirtiest webs of narrative that I've ever, it was always shocking to me.
以它自己的方式令人愉悦。

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:56.880
Delightful in its own way.
这些是精心详细描绘的，比如浪漫的，像言情小说那样的小说类型，比如，这是通往这一刻的步骤。

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:04.920
Were these like elaborately detailed, like romantic, like a romance novel type of like, here's the, here's the steps that led up to this moment.
我们只是让你知道，但这只是我和乔希之间的事。

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:07.320
We just let you know, but this is between Josh and I.
你知道我的意思吗？

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:08.159
You know what I mean?
这是一个特定的时刻，只有我和乔希确切知道在当时的情境下说的是什么，以及它是如何表达的。

00:45:08.159 --> 00:45:16.079
This is a moment in time, and only Josh and I know exactly what was said in the context of what was said and how it was delivered.
但现在你知道发生了什么。

00:45:16.079 --> 00:45:17.400
But now you know what was happening.
你的想象力可以自由驰骋了。

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:18.840
Your imagination is free to wander.
当他在床醒来的时候，任何时候，或者你知道的。

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:22.119
As he was waking up in bed at any time, or you know.
我完全不知道。

00:45:22.119 --> 00:45:23.440
I had no idea.
原来那是……

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:24.280
That's what...
我们坐在那边，在视频监视器那边50英尺外，完全不知道发生了什么。

00:45:24.280 --> 00:45:28.800
We're sitting over there and 50 feet over there in the video village having no idea what's going on.
你声音太小了，我们在里面根本听不见。

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:31.760
You said so quietly, we couldn't hear it in here.
这太棒了。

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:32.880
This is the best.
我们在这里为《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝爆出大新闻了。

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:35.239
We're breaking news here for how I met your mother fans.
我们正在打破新闻头条。

00:45:35.239 --> 00:45:37.079
We are breaking news headlines.
这就是他们想知道的东西。

00:45:37.079 --> 00:45:38.079
This is the stuff they want to know.
我觉得这是吸睛头条。

00:45:38.079 --> 00:45:40.360
I think headlines spinning headlines.
而且我也极其容易破防。

00:45:40.360 --> 00:45:42.920
And I'm also incredibly breakable.
比如我不是，我很经不起逗。

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:45.199
Like I'm not, I'm easy to break.
但我想可能在一些花絮里，你看到我笑的时候，大概就是在对某种事情做出反应。

00:45:45.199 --> 00:45:50.679
But I think probably in some of the blooper reels when you see me laughing is probably in response to something.
很有可能，如果你看到我们在床上。

00:45:50.679 --> 00:45:52.639
It might be, if you see us in bed.
现在我看到这个就会明白了。

00:45:52.639 --> 00:45:54.079
Now I'll know when I see this.
这就是为什么……

00:45:54.079 --> 00:45:54.920
Yeah.
对。

00:45:54.920 --> 00:45:55.760
You could lose it.
你可能会笑场。

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:57.440
You could lose it in a blooper.
你可能会在NG花絮里笑场。

00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:02.440
It took nothing for me to totally lose it.
我完全笑场简直不费吹灰之力。

00:46:02.719 --> 00:46:04.320
Well, it wasn't only that.
嗯，不仅如此。

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:11.320
Sometimes you would get the thing where you needed 12 minutes to recover, like you couldn't pull yourself together.
有时你会遇到那种需要12分钟才能恢复的情况，就好像你根本无法平复心情。

00:46:12.519 --> 00:46:13.360
Yeah.
对。

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:14.360
A hundred percent.
百分之百。

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:15.360
And I, and I.
而且我，而且我。

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:16.519
That's the best though.
不过那才是最棒的。

00:46:16.519 --> 00:46:17.360
It's such a great feeling.
那感觉真是太棒了。

00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:18.719
It's a cycle though, right?
不过这是一个循环，对吧？

00:46:18.719 --> 00:46:21.880
Cause it happens and then you're like, don't laugh.
因为事情发生了，然后你会想，别笑。

00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:23.639
You're like telling yourself, don't laugh.
你会一直在心里对自己说，别笑。

00:46:23.639 --> 00:46:29.880
and then you enter into a phase which you're like, I'm so unprofessional and you're sort of like self-shaming.
然后你会进入这样一个阶段：觉得自己太不专业了，然后开始有点自我羞耻。

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:35.199
Like I'm the youngest one here and they all know what they're doing and I can't not laugh.
就像“我是这里年纪最小的，他们都知道自己在干什么，而我却控制不住自己不笑”。

00:46:35.199 --> 00:46:38.159
And Jason would break me real quick.
杰森总能很快把我逗笑。

00:46:38.159 --> 00:46:48.800
Like Jason would say, also Jason would always be like kind of singing around me and like, and I'd have to be like, oh, it's so annoying.
比如杰森会说，而且杰森总是喜欢在我身边哼歌之类的，然后我就不得不说，哦，这太烦人了。

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:50.599
You know, it's easy when you're like laugh.
你知道的，当你笑的时候，一切都很顺理成章。

00:46:50.599 --> 00:46:53.719
There's something that makes sense that you're laughing about.
你笑总是有原因的。

00:46:53.719 --> 00:46:54.960
What was the episode?
那是哪一集？

00:46:54.960 --> 00:47:01.460
I think it's in first season, but where Marshall had these pants that Lily wanted him to get rid of.
我想是在第一季里，马歇尔有一条莉莉想让他扔掉的裤子。

00:47:01.460 --> 00:47:04.420
And they were like big flowy pattern pants.
那是那种宽大飘逸的有花纹的裤子。

00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:07.260
Oh, they were kind of like empty hammer pants, weren't they?
哦，有点像那种宽松的哈伦裤，对吧？

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:08.099
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:47:08.099 --> 00:47:12.659
And Jason did a dance through the room, living room going, eh?
然后杰森在客厅里扭着跳了一段舞，一边走一边喊：呃？

00:47:12.659 --> 00:47:13.500
Eh?
呃？

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:14.340
So good.
太棒了。

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:16.980
He also broke so much.
他自己也经常笑场。

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:22.019
And that's when it was the hardest, is when he was doing something that broke all of us.
而且那是经历最艰难的时候，就是当他在做某种能把我们所有人逗笑的动作时。

00:47:22.019 --> 00:47:22.860
Yeah.
对。

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:27.539
And then he would be anticipating all of his breaking, and then he would laugh.
然后他会预料到自己又要笑场了，于是他自己就先笑了。

00:47:27.539 --> 00:47:30.340
I would say Neil had the biggest poker face.
我觉得尼尔的扑克脸最厉害。

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:35.019
Like, Neil would be the, he would the last to break, but he would sometimes break, but he was the last.
尼尔总是那个坚持到最后、最晚笑场的人，虽然他有时也会笑，但他是最后一个。

00:47:35.019 --> 00:47:39.260
No, if you made Neil break, you were like, oh, I guess I'm doing something very funny.
不，如果你能把尼尔逗笑，你就会觉得，哦，我猜我刚才做的事一定非常搞笑。

00:47:39.260 --> 00:47:40.739
Kobe Josh and I have talked about this though.
科比、乔希和我讨论过这个。

00:47:40.739 --> 00:47:45.260
Like, you guys, like some of what you're describing went into the show.
比如，你们刚才描述的一些情景其实都放进了剧集里。

00:47:45.260 --> 00:47:47.059
We wouldn't not use those takes.
我们不会剪掉那些镜头。

00:47:47.059 --> 00:47:52.239
We would sometimes use those moments because you guys were unafraid of laughing at each other.
我们有时会保留那些时刻，因为你们并不害怕在镜头前互嘲。

00:47:52.239 --> 00:47:56.480
And I think it's part of what created that feeling of this is an authentic friend group.
我认为这也是营造出“这是一个真实的朋友圈”这种感觉的原因之一。

00:47:56.480 --> 00:48:02.760
I was just texting Josh this last night, I was like, there's something about watching, like we're watching the episode, the duel to talk about that one up next, after you.
我昨晚还给乔希发短信说，看剧的感觉真的很奇妙，比如我们正在看那一集，等下就聊那一集，接着你们的后面。

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:05.000
This is our special Kobe, this is the Kobe episode.
这是我们的特别节目，科比，这是科比专属集。

00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:07.000
We're not talking about any specific episodes.
我们不是在聊具体哪一集。

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:10.300
No, no, we wanted to just talk to you, which is the best.
不，不，我们就是想跟你聊聊，这感觉太棒了。

00:48:10.300 --> 00:48:15.280
But I was watching that episode and I was like, God, you just buy that these people are friends.
但我看那一集的时候就在想，天哪，你真的会相信这些人是好朋友。

00:48:15.280 --> 00:48:16.920
They really seem fond of each other.
他们看起来真的很喜欢彼此。

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:22.639
And I think part of it is the process kind of be gapped that by, we didn't have an audience.
我想部分原因在于我们的制作流程巧妙地避开了这一点——我们没有现场观众。

00:48:22.639 --> 00:48:28.840
You weren't putting it on for a big, the friends actors were putting it on for 500 people in bleachers every day, every show.
你们不需要刻意去迎合大众。《老友记》的演员们每天、每场演出都要面对看台上的五百名观众表演。

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:29.800
We weren't.
而我们没有。

00:48:29.800 --> 00:48:31.119
And you guys had each other.
你们拥有彼此。

00:48:31.119 --> 00:48:33.360
And you guys making each other laugh and the crew laugh.
你们互相逗笑，也把剧组工作人员逗笑。

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:35.599
That's what our laughs were.
那就是我们的笑声来源。

00:48:35.599 --> 00:48:36.679
And we left them in the cut.
而且我们把这些都保留在了正片里。

00:48:36.679 --> 00:48:40.679
And I don't think, I feel like that's part of what made the show work in a weird way.
我觉得，某种程度上这也是这部剧之所以成功的原因之一。

00:48:40.679 --> 00:48:42.079
Yeah, I would agree with that.
对，我非常赞同。

00:48:42.079 --> 00:48:59.760
And I think because, again, it creates this intimacy because I remember doing scenes and there were certain crew members laugh that I would listen for, mostly in camera because they were the closest and they were obviously paying attention.
而且我认为，正因为如此，它创造了一种亲密感。我记得在拍戏时，有些工作人员的笑声是我会特别去捕捉的，主要是在摄像机附近的人，因为他们离得最近，而且他们显然一直在关注着。

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:05.500
And you would kind of hear them laugh and then you would kind of lean into that laugh.
你会听到他们的笑声，然后你就会顺着那种笑声继续发挥。

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:18.400
But it was when you're doing a show like Friends and you're doing that, but it's for an audience of people, I think that that energy you're putting out as an actor is bigger because you're like, oh, they're really like this.
但当你拍像《老友记》这样的剧时，你是面对着现场观众在表演，我觉得作为演员你释放出的能量会更大，因为你会觉得，哦，他们真的很吃这一套。

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:28.599
But when you're just doing it for like, just look on Jane, you know, it's a different, they're laughing because they know what you're doing is actually not Robin.
但当你只是为了——就看简的表情，你知道的，这是完全不同的氛围，他们笑是因为他们知道你做的事情其实根本不是罗宾。

00:49:28.599 --> 00:49:35.199
They know like what you're doing is kind of a bit of a nod to whatever bit we were doing on set.
他们知道你做的事其实是对我们在片场玩的某个梗的一点致敬。

00:49:35.199 --> 00:49:38.039
And that kind of creates a wonderful energy.
这种氛围会产生一种美妙的能量。

00:49:38.039 --> 00:49:46.679
I think that we talked about, and I remember we made this decision kind of as a group that we hated when in a comedy, people were saying hilarious things and no one laughs.
我想我们曾讨论过，而且我记得我们作为团队做过这样一个决定：我们讨厌在喜剧里，大家说著极其搞笑的话，却没有人笑。

00:49:46.679 --> 00:49:51.079
So we made the decision like, no, if you're trying to make your friends laugh, we should laugh.
所以我们决定：不，如果你在试图逗笑你的朋友，我们就应该笑。

00:49:51.079 --> 00:50:09.840
And I'm Sue Fetterman cut in so many reaction shots of people being amused by, and I also think not having the audience, you know, eating candy and amped up, it kept us more like small scale indie film, like, I mean, how I met your mother could get very broad, but it was also like fairly subtle acting for us sitcom, you know?
苏·费特曼（Sue Fetterman）剪辑了大量的反应镜头，展示大家被逗乐的样子。而且我也觉得，没有现场观众（吃着糖果、情绪亢奋），让我们更像是拍小成本独立电影——当然，《老友记》的风格可能会很宽泛，但对我们这部情景喜剧来说，表演其实也相当细腻，对吧？

00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:15.159
Two of you, just while we have the two of you together, I just wanna pay you another compliment to make Kobe's skin crawl.
正好你们俩都在，趁着我们把你们两个聚在一起，我想再夸你们一句，让科比浑身起鸡皮疙瘩。

00:50:15.159 --> 00:50:24.760
Kobe, you will, so this episode that we're doing right now will sort of be what Alec, I think seven or eight hymnium episodes in or something like that, right?
科比，你看，我们现在正在聊的这一集，大概是阿莱克（Alec），我想大概是《老友记》播到第七集或第八集左右吧，或者差不多？

00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:34.400
We were about there, it's about not quite halfway into the season, we just watched and talked about the slutty pumpkin episode, Kobe.
我们大概拍到那里了，差不多快到季中期了。我们刚刚看过并讨论了“万圣节南瓜”那一集，科比。

00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:45.599
There's a scene at the end of the slutty pumpkin where Robin has sort of failed to connect with this guy she's dating down in the bar while Ted is up waiting for the slutty pumpkin at the rooftop Halloween party.
在“万圣节南瓜”快结束的时候有一个场景：罗宾在酒吧里和她约会的那个男人沟通失败，而泰德则在屋顶的万圣节派对上苦苦等待那个“南瓜”。

00:50:45.599 --> 00:50:50.730
And Robin ends up kind of feeling like the guy leaves her.
最后罗宾觉得那个男人把她甩了。

00:50:51.210 --> 00:50:55.530
The guy says, I sense you are not ready to connect on this level and be in a relationship.
那个男人说：“我感觉你还没准备好在心灵深处进行交流并建立恋爱关系。”

00:50:55.530 --> 00:50:58.610
And he leaves and sort of leaves her wondering something about herself.
然后他离开了，留下一脸自我怀疑的罗宾。

00:50:58.610 --> 00:51:07.250
And if she kind of has this moment where she needs a word of advice about life and she goes and the tables kind of turn she kind of goes to Ted.
接着她迎来了一个时刻，她需要一些关于人生的忠告，于是风水轮流转，她走向了泰德。

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:20.690
She goes, she climbs up to this roof and Ted sitting there having seemed very absurd to be waiting for the slutty pumpkin like Linus and Charlie Brown, but he has this wisdom that she needs in that moment where she comes to Ted and she says, how do you do it, Ted?
她走过去，爬上了这个屋顶，泰德正坐在那儿，苦苦等待那个万圣节南瓜，看起来就像《花生漫画》里的莱纳斯和查理·布朗一样荒谬，但在那个时刻，当罗宾走向泰德并对他说“你是怎么做到的，泰德？”时，他展现出了她所需要的智慧。

00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:29.769
How do you sit here and have faith that this person, that love is possible essentially, that this person's gonna show up, this person who represents hope and like, how do you do it?
“你怎么能坐在这里，坚信这个人——坚信爱是可能的，坚信这个代表希望的人会出现，你怎么做到的？”

00:51:29.769 --> 00:51:32.409
Is there something broken in me that I can't seem to do it?
“难道我身上有什么地方坏掉了，所以做不到这一点吗？”

00:51:32.409 --> 00:51:34.010
Why don't I, why can't I do that?
“为什么我不能？为什么我做不到那样？”

00:51:34.010 --> 00:51:35.489
Why can't I feel those things?
“为什么我感受不到那些情绪？”

00:51:35.489 --> 00:51:46.289
It's such a great scene between the two of you And such an interesting, I think, you know, that six episodes in or something, where Robin goes from being sort of this object, we're talking about like the tantalizing object.
你们俩之间的这场戏太棒了！而且我觉得这是一个非常有趣的转折——在拍了六集左右的时候，罗宾从一个单纯被追求的对象——我们之前讨论过的那个诱人的目标——

00:51:46.289 --> 00:51:49.530
This thing Ted is chasing has decided she's gonna be the answer to all his problems.
成了泰德一直在追逐、并决定让她来解决自己所有问题的人。

00:51:49.530 --> 00:52:01.809
And Robin, we've, you and we figured out how to dimensionalize Robin between the pilot and six episodes in, enough that already we're kind of peeling back the onion a little bit and going like, what is going on underneath for Robin?
而罗宾，你和我们一起在试播集到第六集之间，成功让罗宾变得丰满立体起来，以至于仅仅播到了这个阶段，我们就已经开始像剥洋葱一样，去探究罗宾内心的真实想法了。

00:52:01.809 --> 00:52:03.010
Who is she really?
她究竟是一个什么样的人？

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:15.329
Talk about that moment or just a moment in general where you started to discover like in that episode, I just was noting Robin is being shown as a flawed dimensional human being, not the object of what Ted's chasing or projecting on her.
聊聊那个时刻，或者整体上你开始发现——比如在那一集中，我注意到罗宾展现出了有缺陷但立体的真实人格，而不是泰德追逐的目标或投射的对象。

00:52:15.329 --> 00:52:16.769
She's her own character.
她有她自己的性格。

00:52:16.769 --> 00:52:21.170
I thought you played that vulnerability so well, and that was sort of the first moment that that happened.
我觉得你把那种脆弱感演绎得太棒了，那可以说是这种转变发生的第一个瞬间。

00:52:21.170 --> 00:52:25.690
Do you remember that scene or that feeling like you were digging into this character a little more?
你还记得那个场景吗？或者那种深入挖掘这个角色的感觉？

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:28.969
I remember that scene a little bit, to be completely honest.
说实话，我对那个场景只有一点点记忆了。

00:52:28.969 --> 00:52:30.929
I haven't re-watched the show.
我没有重温过这部剧。

00:52:30.929 --> 00:52:32.769
The last time I've seen every episode.
我最后一次看每一集是很久以前了。

00:52:32.769 --> 00:52:36.690
There's a podcast that you can re-watch along with if you want to find it.
如果你想重温的话，有一个播客可以边看边听。

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:37.849
It's called How We Made Your Mother.
它叫《我们是如何创造你母亲的》（How We Made Your Mother）。

00:52:37.849 --> 00:52:38.469
Okay, great.
好的，太棒了。

00:52:38.469 --> 00:52:41.048
But I just find it anywhere that you get podcasts.
但我只是在任何能收听播客的地方都能找到它。

00:52:41.048 --> 00:52:43.369
Is that right anywhere you get your podcasts?
真的是在任何能收听播客的地方吗？

00:52:43.369 --> 00:52:44.444
You get your podcasts.
只要能听播客的地方都有。

00:52:44.444 --> 00:52:46.691
You can just tell them I'll send you the link.
你跟他们说一声，我把链接发给你。

00:52:46.691 --> 00:52:48.010
You can subscribe probably.
你大概可以订阅。

00:52:48.010 --> 00:52:50.390
It works like every other podcast.
它和其他播客一样。

00:52:50.390 --> 00:52:51.650
There's really no.
真的没什么特别的。

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:52.150
Okay.
好吧。

00:52:52.150 --> 00:52:52.650
Okay.
好吧。

00:52:52.650 --> 00:52:53.150
Yeah.
对。

00:52:53.150 --> 00:52:54.970
Just like every other one.
就像其他所有事情一样。

00:52:54.970 --> 00:52:55.600
Um, yeah.
嗯，对。

00:52:55.600 --> 00:53:11.770
I mean, I remember that sort of push and pull of Robin at the beginning of the season because, you know, she's, she's coming out of thing and she wants to be single and she's in this new city and she's very career-driven, you know?
我的意思是，我记得在第一季刚开始时，罗宾身上那种推拉的感觉，因为你知道的，她刚经历了一些事，她想单身，身处一个新城市，而且非常注重事业，你知道的？

00:53:11.770 --> 00:53:17.160
And I think it's like it's a battle between the head and the heart, you know?
我觉得这就好比是理智与情感之间的较量，你知道吗？

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:31.440
She's going, you know, obviously I want this career and that requires me to be selfish and that requires me to not really have emotional availability to people because I'm just, you know, this is my goal.
她在想，你知道的，很显然我想要这份事业，而这就需要我自私一点，需要我在某种程度上对别人没有过多的情感投入，因为我满脑子想的都是，这就是我的目标。

00:53:31.440 --> 00:53:35.220
But I think she meets Ted and he is just such a catch.
但我觉得她遇到了泰德，而他简直太完美了。

00:53:34.769 --> 00:53:39.269
And she's asking herself, why am I pushing this away?
她在问自己，我为什么要推开这一切？

00:53:39.269 --> 00:53:41.929
Why am I having a hard time being intimate?
为什么我对亲密关系感到这么难？

00:53:41.929 --> 00:53:44.489
And I don't think that she has really thought about that.
我觉得她以前并没有真正思考过这个问题。

00:53:44.489 --> 00:53:52.769
I mean, if we think about how she grew up and sort of we get into that and further seasons the kind of father she had and you know, not to get to.
我是说，如果我们回想她的成长经历，并且在接下来的几季中慢慢深入了解她经历过的父亲类型，你知道的，虽然不是说要……

00:53:52.769 --> 00:53:55.090
And we didn't know all of that at those early moments.
而且我们在刚开始的时候并不了解所有这些。

00:53:55.090 --> 00:53:56.929
And we were still found a way to play it.
我们还是找到了方法来演绎。

00:53:56.929 --> 00:54:01.769
And then we kind of, we figured it out retroactively because of how you played it.
然后我们差不多是根据你的表演，事后才把这些给琢磨透的。

00:54:01.769 --> 00:54:02.769
I think.
我觉得是这样。

00:54:02.769 --> 00:54:03.769
Yeah.
对。

00:54:03.769 --> 00:54:12.250
And I also want to just talk about Josh for a bit, which is like Josh is so emotionally intelligent.
而且我还想聊聊乔希，就是乔希在情感上真的非常成熟。

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:17.650
He's obviously intelligent, but there is a depth to Josh as a human.
他显然很聪明，但作为一个真正的人，乔希身上有一种深邃感。

00:54:17.650 --> 00:54:27.650
And he is so, he's so ready to listen and just gives the most wonderful advice.
他非常、非常愿意倾听，总能给出最棒的建议。

00:54:27.650 --> 00:54:53.570
And I feel like even when we first met, if we want to go back to that time specifically, you know, she's a 22-year-old girl who's like, you know, and it was so wonderful to be able to partner with somebody who I could talk to about life stuff and also have such a depth of knowledge about the emotional, about humans in general, about relationships.
我觉得即使在我们刚见面的时候，如果我们特别回到那个时期，你知道的，她当时只是个22岁的女孩，就那种，而且能和这样一个人搭档真的很棒，我可以跟他聊生活中的事，同时他对情感、对整个人类、对人际关系又有如此深厚的认知。

00:54:53.570 --> 00:55:00.530
And I think that that was probably what was happening in that scene at that time.
我想那大概就是那个场景中正在发生的事情。

00:55:00.530 --> 00:55:04.530
First off, the Cubs, that was so lovely.
首先，小熊队，那真是太美妙了。

00:55:04.530 --> 00:55:05.530
Thanks.
谢谢。

00:55:05.530 --> 00:55:10.650
I'm so moved by that, but I also, I think I was 29 when we did the pilot.
我对此非常感动，不过我也觉得，我们拍试播集时我大概29岁。

00:55:10.650 --> 00:55:15.929
Maybe you were like 23 and I was 30 by the time we were actually doing it, or does that sound about right?
也许你当时23岁左右，而我们真正开拍的时候我已经30岁了，或者说大概是这样？

00:55:15.929 --> 00:55:21.969
But, you know, it's funny because I was like older than you, but this was also new for me.
不过，你知道的，这挺有意思的，因为我比你年长，但这对当时的我也很新鲜。

00:55:21.969 --> 00:56:03.336
I had never been on a big long-running TV show, I had done a bunch of pilots and so we were both in the trenches together and I think one of the ways I sometimes deal with my discomfort is I try to like be advicey and sage like and you know but you were I think we were figuring this thing out together and I have such a strong memory of seeing the first you've been the first time like walking down the hall when we were about to test together and I think that, you know, people have always remarked on our chemistry to the point where people insist that I was in love with you.
我从来没演过大型的长寿电视剧，我拍过一堆试播集，所以我们俩当时都在摸着石头过河，而且我觉得我有时面对不适的一种方式，就是假装自己很有建议性、像个智者一样，你知道的，但你——我觉得我们是一起在把这事琢磨明白的，我脑海中有一个特别深刻的记忆，就是第一次看到你、第一次当我们准备一起去试镜时走在走廊上，我觉得，你知道的，人们总是对我们的化学反应赞不绝口，甚至有人坚信我当时爱上了你。

00:56:03.336 --> 00:56:15.125
And I think you know that that was not true, but I did always feel like you and I had like, I don't know like we met on the first day of basic training or something.
我想你知道那并不是真的，但我确实总觉得你和我之间有种，我不知道，就像我们在新兵训练营的第一天相遇一样。

00:56:15.125 --> 00:56:23.769
Like I felt like we were in the military together and we went through like this life changer together, like side by side.
我感觉我们就好像一起参了军，并肩经历了一场彻底改变人生的大事。

00:56:23.769 --> 00:56:27.650
And I, you know, sometimes people would ask about chemistry, did you ever discuss chemistry?
而且，你知道的，有时人们会问及化学反应的事，你们讨论过化学反应吗？

00:56:27.650 --> 00:56:29.250
And I'd be like, God, no.
我就会说，天哪，没有。

00:56:29.250 --> 00:56:32.369
Like I don't, that would be the worst thing you could ever do.
我觉得，那绝对是你能做的最糟糕的事情。

00:56:32.369 --> 00:56:42.409
Like I just had a sense that scenes with you and I were gonna be really honest and they were gonna be really thoughtful and we were gonna listen really well together.
我只是有一种直觉，我和你的对手戏会非常真诚，会非常深思熟虑，而且我们两个会配合得非常默契，懂得如何倾听彼此。

00:56:42.409 --> 00:56:46.610
And I also thought, you know, Jordana made this point about your character, especially in the first season.
而且我还觉得，你知道的，乔丹娜对你的角色提出了这样一个观点，尤其是在第一季中。

00:56:46.610 --> 00:56:58.502
There's a psychological term called tantalizing object, which is like the thing that like you really want, but it's just out of reach, and you can't quite have it, and we all have that romantically.
心理学上有一个词叫“诱人客体”，意思就是你特别想要、但又偏偏够不着的东西，你无法真正拥有它，我们在浪漫感情中都会有这种感觉。

00:56:58.502 --> 00:57:06.250
And I think one thing that I think is fascinating is you met Taryn right before we started this thing, and you're still with him.
我觉得有一点很迷人，那就是我们在开始拍这部戏之前不久，你遇到了泰伦，而且你们现在还在一起。

00:57:06.250 --> 00:57:20.230
So you yourself were not, we didn't have these attachment theory terms when we were doing the show, but like Craig and I have talked that Ted has an anxious attachment wound, And Robin has an avoidant attachment wound.
所以你自己其实并没有——我们在拍这部剧的时候并没有这些依恋理论的概念，但我和克雷格聊过，泰德有焦虑型依恋创伤，而罗宾有回避型依恋创伤。

00:57:20.230 --> 00:57:29.829
But in real life, actually, we were reversed because you were in this really serious long-term relationship that you're still in.
但在现实生活中，实际上我们俩正好相反，因为你当时正处于一段非常稳定的长期恋情中，而且直到现在你们还在一块。

00:57:29.829 --> 00:57:37.070
And I was actually, as you remember from many of our off-stage talks, really confused about relationships, like how I couldn't stay in them.
而我实际上，正如你从我们的许多幕后交谈中所记得的那样，对人际关系感到非常迷茫，比如我怎么就留不住感情。

00:57:37.070 --> 00:57:42.349
I didn't know how long if I wanted to be in them, if I wanted to ever get married, I've worked through all that.
我不知道自己想谈多久的恋爱，不知道自己是否想维系下去，是否想过结婚，这些我都已经克服了。

00:57:42.349 --> 00:57:54.269
But I find it fascinating that we were both navigating like love and romance at that time in our lives, but from totally different perspectives than our characters.
但我发现很奇妙的是，我们当时都在探索爱与浪漫，但其视角却与我们的角色截然不同。

00:57:54.269 --> 00:58:09.349
But I don't know, there was just something about, when people ask like if we're friends, like with the cast, I say sure, but it's actually family is a better analogy because you don't get to choose your family.
不过我不知道，就是有这样一种感觉，当人们问我们是不是朋友时，比如演员之间，我会说是的，但实际上用“家人”来形容更贴切，因为你没法选择自己的家人。

00:58:09.349 --> 00:58:10.869
You're just thrown together.
你们只是被随机凑到了一起。

00:58:10.869 --> 00:58:14.150
and you have to kind of make this ecosystem work.
而你必须让这个生态系统正常运转。

00:58:14.150 --> 00:58:22.309
And, you know, there were, set was different when you and I had a one-on-one scene than it was five of us at the bar.
而且，你知道的，当你我有一场双人戏时，片场的感觉和我们五个人在酒吧里时是不一样的。

00:58:22.309 --> 00:58:32.750
Or you and Neil together, or me and Jason and Ali, like there were so many different configurations that changed the energy of what the day was gonna be like.
或者你和尼尔在一起，或者我和杰森、阿里在一起，有太多不同的组合，它们改变了那一天的活力和氛围。

00:58:32.750 --> 00:58:43.579
But I do, you know, in some ways like if it's the Wizard of Oz, like you were the Scarecrow for me, like you were like the first person I met on the journey.
但我的确，你知道的，在某种程度上就像《绿野仙踪》那样，对我来说你就像是稻草人，就像是这段旅程中我遇到的第一个人。

00:58:43.579 --> 00:58:45.340
And I just like, I love you very much.
我只是觉得，我非常爱你。

00:58:45.340 --> 00:58:47.960
And I just, I just want to say that.
我只想说这些。

00:58:47.960 --> 00:58:49.320
Say it, buddy.
说出来吧，伙计。

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:50.239
Oh, are you guys?
哦，你们俩？

00:58:50.239 --> 00:58:51.079
I love you both.
我爱你们两个。

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:53.480
It's, what a gift.
这真是，多么棒的恩赐啊。

00:58:53.480 --> 00:58:55.880
How lucky were, how lucky were slash are we?
我们有多幸运，过去和现在都有多幸运？

00:58:55.880 --> 00:58:56.719
I mean, just.
我是说，简直了。

00:58:56.719 --> 00:58:57.534
Oh, lucky.
哦，太幸运了。

00:58:57.534 --> 00:58:58.920
We were so lucky.
我们太幸运了。

00:58:58.920 --> 00:59:03.679
Like it's so, it's such a, it was such an amazing time.
就像，这真的是一段太棒的时光了。

00:59:04.639 --> 00:59:06.280
And just, I miss it.
而且，我很想念它。

00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:11.739
Like I don't like, I don't really miss people go like, What do you miss most of the show and of like the people?
就像，我不——当人们问“你最想念这部剧的什么”时，我会说“当然是人啊”。

00:59:11.739 --> 00:59:13.739
Like being able to just go in.
就像能够那样随心所欲地去工作。

00:59:13.739 --> 00:59:20.019
And I think you've touched upon this in your podcast already, which is like life happened.
我想你在你的播客中也已经提到过这一点，那就是生活一直在发生。

00:59:20.019 --> 00:59:25.019
Like people got married, people met their partners, people divorced their partners.
比如有人结婚了，有人遇到了伴侣，有人和伴侣离婚了。

00:59:25.019 --> 00:59:26.900
We have people pass away, you know?
我们还有人离世了，你知道吗？

00:59:26.900 --> 00:59:27.739
It was like.
就好像是。

00:59:27.739 --> 00:59:28.579
Yeah.
对。

00:59:28.579 --> 00:59:29.820
And we all went through some shit.
我们都经历了一些破事。

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.820
We all went through some journeys and some shit.
我们都经历了一段段心路历程和各种破事。

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:32.659
Yeah.
对。

00:59:32.659 --> 00:59:33.659
We really did.
我们真的经历过。

00:59:33.659 --> 00:59:37.260
And so it's, it's, that made it in there too.
所以这一切，也都融入其中了。

00:59:37.260 --> 00:59:39.539
It's just, it was like a lifetime.
这感觉就像是一整辈子。

00:59:39.539 --> 00:59:42.880
And when I even say like, we, and people ask like, how many seasons did you do?
每当我甚至说“我们”的时候，当人们问“你们拍了多少季”？

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:44.840
I was like, we did nine seasons.
我会说，我们拍了九季。

00:59:44.840 --> 00:59:46.500
We did nine years.
我们拍了九年。

00:59:46.500 --> 00:59:49.380
Like, call it a decade of our life, call it a decade.
把它叫做我们生命中的十年吧，就叫十年。

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:50.219
And we were all living life.
而我们都在好好生活。

00:59:50.219 --> 00:59:57.199
The other thing though, I think someone to think about is like, everyone sees 22 to 24 minutes of that week that we spent together.
不过还有一件事，我觉得可以想想，那就是每个人看到的只是我们共同度过的那个星期的22到24分钟。

00:59:57.199 --> 00:59:58.780
But how many hours were we together?
但我们到底在一起度过了多少小时？

00:59:58.780 --> 01:00:02.219
Like, most of our life was happening not on screen.
我们的大部分生活其实并没有发生在屏幕上。

01:00:02.219 --> 01:00:04.539
Like, even on set life.
甚至包括在片场的生活。

01:00:04.539 --> 01:00:07.579
And people think like, oh, no, it's the five of you.
人们以为，哦，不，就是你们五个人。

01:00:07.579 --> 01:00:11.340
And we try to remind people on the podcast, no, it was 75 of us.
我们试图在播客中提醒大家，不，是我们这75个人。

01:00:11.340 --> 01:00:13.739
There were so many people around.
周围有那么多的工作人员。

01:00:13.739 --> 01:00:18.219
And so many people you get really close to.
有那么多让你真正亲近的人。

01:00:18.219 --> 01:00:23.460
Jen, Turkey didn't even do my makeup, but we're still in touch.
詹，虽然土耳其（昵称）甚至都没给我化过妆，但我们现在还保持着联系。

01:00:23.460 --> 01:00:27.340
I just adore her and I gave a toast at her wedding.
我太崇拜她了，我还曾在她的婚礼上致祝酒词。

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:42.789
You really get woven into people's lives in a way that is not, the public's never gonna get how intimate and how sweet a well-functioning set can be.
你真的会以一种外人永远无法理解的方式交织进别人的生活中，公众永远不会明白一个运转良好的剧组可以有多亲密、多温馨。

01:00:42.989 --> 01:00:46.789
But they do, I think they don't know it, but they feel it in the show.
但他们确实——我觉得他们虽然说不上来，但能在剧中感受到这一点。

01:00:46.789 --> 01:00:47.789
Because it shows up on screen.
因为这在银幕上展现出来了。

01:00:47.789 --> 01:00:49.170
That made it into this show.
那种氛围融入了这部剧里。

01:00:49.170 --> 01:00:54.469
I don't know that it makes, it's not like that on every creative endeavor, but this one, it just filtered in.
我不知道是不是每个创意工作都是这样的，但对于这部剧，它就是自然而然地渗透进去了。

01:00:54.469 --> 01:01:00.949
I just had this sense memory of this feeling of walking down to set, as I'm looking at the two of you loving you and missing you, but guys both.
我刚刚突然有了一种画面记忆，那种走向片场的感觉，当我看着你们俩、爱着你们、想念你们时，但是你们两个。

01:01:00.949 --> 01:01:11.710
and like, just this feeling of missing working with you, I mean, walking down the set, knowing I was gonna get to shoot, let's say that scene in the slutty pumpkin, the two of you up on the roof.
那种想念和你们一起工作的感觉，我是说，走在去片场的路上，知道我要去拍戏了，比方说拍“荡妇南瓜”那一集，你们两个在屋顶上的那场戏。

01:01:11.710 --> 01:01:21.269
Or that scene at the end of episode two of the Purple Giraffe where Robin sort of is saying, essentially, Kobe, what we started this podcast with, which is, can I be part of your group?
或者第二集《紫色长颈鹿》结尾的那场戏，罗宾差不多在说，本质上说，科比，也就是我们这期播客开始时提到的，我能加入你们的小团体吗？

01:01:21.269 --> 01:01:26.670
She's saying, I know I'm not saying I'm gonna date you, but it's too bad because I don't have friends here.
她在说，我知道——我不是说我要跟你约会，但这太糟了，因为我在这里没有朋友。

01:01:26.670 --> 01:01:32.360
And there's this wonderful moment where Ted says, come down with us at the bar tonight, you know?
然后有一个非常美妙的时刻，泰德说，今晚跟我们一起去酒吧吧，你知道吗？

01:01:32.360 --> 01:01:37.159
It was like, he said, maybe in a couple months, we'll talk and she's like, and Robin's heartbroken for second, she knows it's not gonna happen.
就像他说的那样，也许过几个月，我们再聊，然后她就——罗宾心碎了一秒钟，她知道这不会发生了。

01:01:37.159 --> 01:01:39.559
Then he says, or tonight, come down, Drake, with us tonight.
然后他说，或者就今晚，下来吧，今晚跟我们一起。

01:01:39.559 --> 01:01:44.440
It's this lovely moment where she's invited into the group and it felt like you being invited into the show.
这是一个美好的时刻，她被邀请加入了这个群体，这就感觉像是你被邀请加入了这部剧。

01:01:44.440 --> 01:01:49.639
And just like, knowing I got to go shoot scenes with the two of you, I mean, what a gift is a writer.
而且就，知道我要和你们两个去拍戏，我的意思是，对一个编剧来说这是多么棒的礼物。

01:01:49.639 --> 01:01:51.760
I know Carter very much felt the same way.
我知道卡特也绝对是这么想的。

01:01:51.760 --> 01:01:55.719
It's just like, I get to go shoot a scene with these two today on that roof.
就像是，今天我能在那个屋顶上和这两位拍戏了。

01:01:55.719 --> 01:01:57.920
It's going to be fucking great.
这他妈绝对会超棒。

